WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.080 --> 00:00:08.200
With DARPA support, we have
developed an interactive system

00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:11.230
for the efficient inspection
of multiple videos recorded

00:00:11.230 --> 00:00:15.070
along spatially similar
paths but at different times.

00:00:15.070 --> 00:00:17.410
The system enables a
human sequence analyst

00:00:17.410 --> 00:00:20.470
to detect differences
between the two videos.

00:00:20.470 --> 00:00:22.600
The system is based on
the techniques described

00:00:22.600 --> 00:00:26.020
in the paper "Video Matching"
by Peter Sand and Seth Teller,

00:00:26.020 --> 00:00:29.230
which appeared in the
proceedings of SIGGRAPH 2004.

00:00:29.230 --> 00:00:32.049
The idea is to determine the
frame of the second video that

00:00:32.049 --> 00:00:35.230
is visually closest to each
frame of the primary video.

00:00:35.230 --> 00:00:37.360
This secondary frame
is then warped to match

00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:39.190
the primary frame
and the difference

00:00:39.190 --> 00:00:41.830
between the two frames is
generated as a false color

00:00:41.830 --> 00:00:42.850
image.

00:00:42.850 --> 00:00:45.550
The sequence analyst can
see at a glance which parts

00:00:45.550 --> 00:00:47.140
of the scene have changed.

00:00:47.140 --> 00:00:50.380
With the addition of
reference tags to each video,

00:00:50.380 --> 00:00:53.350
we can search for matching
frames far more efficiently

00:00:53.350 --> 00:00:55.270
and can flag regions
where differences

00:00:55.270 --> 00:00:57.700
exist in a map-based interface.

00:00:57.700 --> 00:01:00.070
This interface quickly
tells the sequence analyst

00:01:00.070 --> 00:01:02.230
which regions of the
map contain areas

00:01:02.230 --> 00:01:05.170
in which the system detected
a substantial difference

00:01:05.170 --> 00:01:08.050
in the scene between the times
that the primary and secondary

00:01:08.050 --> 00:01:10.910
videos were acquired.

00:01:10.910 --> 00:01:14.150
Here is an example of a clear
difference found by our system.

00:01:14.150 --> 00:01:16.970
The system saw that these trash
cans in the secondary video

00:01:16.970 --> 00:01:20.030
were not present when the
primary video was recorded.

00:01:20.030 --> 00:01:22.060
It flagged this segment
as a difference,

00:01:22.060 --> 00:01:23.630
and the trash cans
appear clearly

00:01:23.630 --> 00:01:25.483
in the difference video.

00:01:25.483 --> 00:01:26.900
One of the strengths
of our system

00:01:26.900 --> 00:01:29.390
is that it can detect
differences that may easily

00:01:29.390 --> 00:01:31.400
escape a human observer.

00:01:31.400 --> 00:01:33.650
In this video, the system
detected two people

00:01:33.650 --> 00:01:35.900
walking down some train tracks.

00:01:35.900 --> 00:01:38.660
It is hard to perceive the
people by eye even when

00:01:38.660 --> 00:01:41.630
comparing the secondary
video to the primary video.

00:01:41.630 --> 00:01:44.690
But the system detected it and
a look at the difference video

00:01:44.690 --> 00:01:47.090
shows the two people clearly.

00:01:47.090 --> 00:01:49.280
The system can also
match videos recorded

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:52.820
in largely featureless
environments such as deserts.

00:01:52.820 --> 00:01:55.640
In this example, a
simulated explosive device

00:01:55.640 --> 00:01:57.350
was buried near a fence.

00:01:57.350 --> 00:01:59.720
Even in this largely
homogeneous video,

00:01:59.720 --> 00:02:01.700
the system can still
spot the difference

00:02:01.700 --> 00:02:03.800
as shown in the
difference video.

00:02:03.800 --> 00:02:06.110
The current system
is only a prototype.

00:02:06.110 --> 00:02:09.139
It serves as a proof of concept
that a system based on computer

00:02:09.139 --> 00:02:12.140
vision techniques can perform
useful change detection

00:02:12.140 --> 00:02:14.570
across long video
sequences acquired

00:02:14.570 --> 00:02:17.620
in a variety of environments.

